INT. RESTAURANT WALK-IN FREEZER - NIGHT

Racks of frozen fish and crates of lemons. Breath fogs. A single bulb swings.

VIC SALERNO, fifties, camel-hair coat, sits on an upturned crate. DOLORES KWAN, forties, apron over a black dress, stands at the door with a revolver held loosely at her side.

VIC
You'd have done better with the knife.

DOLORES
The knife is for fish.

VIC
(a thin smile)
I taught you that.

She lowers the gun an inch.

DOLORES
You taught me the books too. Both sets.

VIC
Then you know why I came.

DOLORES
I know why you think you did.

They speak at the same moment.

VIC
It was never personal.

DOLORES ^
It was always personal.

A long beat. Vic stands, slow, rubbing his hands against the cold.

VIC
Open the door, Dolores. I'm freezing.

DOLORES
It locks from the outside, Vic.

She sets the revolver on a shelf of frozen lemons, steps out, and the heavy door thuds shut behind her.
